Lesson 12 — Lab: AI Setup and Prompt Engineering Exercises

You will finish this lab with a small on-prem AI stack running on your own hardware — Ollama, ChromaDB, Open WebUI — plus four Python tools that read Nmap XML, HTTP responses, and finding YAML files and produce pentest-ready summaries.

Everything runs locally so you can use it in client-confidential engagements without leaking data to a hosted model. The next time a customer objects to sending logs to OpenAI or Anthropic, you will already own the alternative.

Module diagram

    Module 05: AI Architecture, Prompt Engineering, and Local AI · Penetration Testing with Kali Linux 1.0 · Red Team Leaders

1. Introduction

Module 05 has walked you through the architectural, operational, and prompt-engineering layers of the modern LLM-augmented offensive-security workflow. Every prior lesson taught a slice in isolation — one lesson on transformer intuition, another on system prompts and few-shot design, another on tokenization and context windows, another on retrieval-augmented generation, another on running local models with Ollama, another on the MCP protocol, another on chain-of-thought and ReAct patterns, another on evaluation and guardrails.

This lesson consolidates the module. You will build four production-grade AI-augmented pentest tools end-to-end. When the lab is finished you should be able to chmod +x each one, drop it into your personal ~/pentest-tools/ git repository, and reach for it during the enumeration, exploitation, and reporting phases of a real engagement. These are not throwaway demos — every subsequent module in the course assumes you have them and asks you to extend them. Module 06 (AI-Augmented Reconnaissance) explicitly wires the triage tool from Challenge 3 into an automated recon pipeline, and Module 12 (AI-Augmented Reporting) reuses the finding writer from Challenge 4.

The four tools are:

    pentest-assistant — a specialized local LLM built on top of llama3.1:8b with a security-focused system prompt, delivered as an Ollama Modelfile.
    ragq — a Python CLI that indexes OWASP Top 10 and a small CVE corpus into ChromaDB, retrieves relevant chunks with nomic-embed-text, and answers pentest questions with a local Ollama model.
    recon-triage — a Python tool that reads Nmap XML, calls a local LLM with a structured triage prompt, and returns hosts ranked by likely attack value as JSON.
    finding-writer — a Python tool that reads a raw finding YAML and drafts a full Markdown finding card with executive summary, technical details, impact, and remediation, calling the Claude Sonnet 5 API with a graceful fallback to a local Ollama model when the API is unavailable.

Each challenge is scoped so the reference implementation runs on Kali 2026 out of the box, and each ends with stretch goals that turn the tool into something you would actually want during a real op. Read the requirements, try to write it yourself, then compare against the reference. Do not just copy — type it, break it, understand it. The rubric at the end of this lesson is how the tools are graded when you submit them at the end of the module.

A brief warning before you start: local models are non-deterministic. Two runs of the same prompt against the same weights will not produce byte-identical output. The grading rubric evaluates behavior, structure, and safety of your outputs — not string matches. When you build tests, assert on shape and content invariants rather than on exact strings.
