# OWASP Top 10 — 2021

## A01:2021 - Broken Access Control
Access control enforces policy such that users cannot act outside their intended
permissions. Failures typically lead to unauthorized information disclosure,
modification, or destruction of data. Common vectors include IDOR, forced
browsing to authenticated pages, JWT tampering, and CORS misconfiguration.

## A02:2021 - Cryptographic Failures
Previously "Sensitive Data Exposure". Root causes are transmitting or storing
sensitive data without encryption, using weak or deprecated algorithms
(MD5, SHA-1, DES, RC4), and mishandling keys. Consequences include credential
theft, session hijack, and regulated-data breach.

## A03:2021 - Injection
Includes SQL, NoSQL, OS command, LDAP, and expression-language injection.
Occurs when untrusted data is sent to an interpreter as part of a command or
query. XSS was folded in for 2021. Mitigations: parameterized queries,
positive server-side input validation, escape output.

## A04:2021 - Insecure Design
A new category focused on missing or ineffective control design. Distinct from
implementation flaws — even a perfectly implemented insecure design is
exploitable. Threat modeling, secure design patterns, and reference
architectures are the countermeasures.

## A05:2021 - Security Misconfiguration
Default accounts, verbose error messages, unnecessary features enabled,
missing security headers, out-of-date software. Cloud services and container
platforms have expanded the surface. XXE joined this category for 2021.

## A06:2021 - Vulnerable and Outdated Components
Using components with known CVEs. Includes direct and transitive dependencies.
Countermeasures: SBOM, dependency pinning, continuous vulnerability scanning
(OWASP Dependency-Check, Snyk, Trivy).

## A07:2021 - Identification and Authentication Failures
Weak credentials, credential stuffing, missing MFA, insecure session
management, exposed session IDs, brute-force endpoints without lockout.
Countermeasures: MFA, strong password policy, credential-stuffing detection,
short session lifetime, secure cookie flags.

## A08:2021 - Software and Data Integrity Failures
CI/CD pipeline compromise, unsigned software updates, deserialization of
untrusted data. Relevant CVEs include Log4Shell-style RCE through crafted
lookup strings and SolarWinds-style supply-chain implants.

## A09:2021 - Security Logging and Monitoring Failures
Missing or inadequate logging, no alerting, no incident response. Increases
dwell time. Detection engineering must cover authentication events, access
control failures, and server-side input validation failures at minimum.

## A10:2021 - Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)
The application fetches a remote resource without validating the user-supplied
URL. Especially dangerous in cloud environments where the metadata service
(169.254.169.254) issues short-lived credentials. Mitigations: allow-list URLs,
disable redirects, network-segment egress.

