7. Independent Challenges

These are open-ended. No reference implementation is provided — that is the point. Pick one at minimum; the ambitious student picks all three. Deliverables go into your ~/pentest-tools/ repo under stretch/ and are graded on the rubric below.
7.1 Fine-tune a LoRA adapter with Unsloth

Assemble a small dataset (100-500 examples) of (pentest_question, ideal_answer) pairs — mine them from your own notes, from HackTricks, from your CTF write-ups. Use Unsloth to LoRA-fine-tune llama3.1:8b on the dataset in a Colab or a local GPU box. Export the resulting adapter, merge it into a GGUF, and package it as an Ollama Modelfile called pentest-assistant-tuned. Compare its output on the three probes from Challenge 1 against the untuned assistant. Document what improved and what regressed.

Grading emphasizes: dataset quality, honest evaluation (including regressions), and reproducibility of the training run.
7.2 Wrap a scanner as an MCP server

Pick a familiar scanner — nmap, nikto, gobuster, nuclei, your own portscan.py from Module 03. Wrap it as a Model Context Protocol server exposing at least three tools (e.g. start_scan, get_status, get_results) and one resource (the raw scan output). Use the reference MCP Python SDK. Prove it works by connecting it to claude (or another MCP client) and driving a scan end-to-end.

Grading emphasizes: schema quality (well-typed tool signatures), correct handling of long-running scans (jobs, not blocking calls), and safety (refusing scans of localhost or of unspecified targets).
7.3 Streamlit UI for the finding writer

Wrap the Challenge 4 tool in a Streamlit UI. The user should be able to: paste a raw finding, select a severity, click "Draft", see the drafted markdown side-by-side with the raw input, edit it inline, and download the final .md. The page must show which model produced the draft (Claude Sonnet 5 vs local fallback) and expose a "regenerate" button.

Grading emphasizes: usability under real conditions (no keyboard-only paths, no un-copyable output), graceful degradation when the API is unreachable, and secret hygiene (no API key in the URL or on-page).
8. Grading Rubric

Each challenge is graded out of 20 points. Total lab: 100 points across the four required challenges (Challenge 1-4) plus up to 20 bonus points for one completed independent challenge from section 7.
Dimension	5 pts (excellent)	3 pts (adequate)	1 pt (weak)
Correctness	Runs against the sample inputs on Kali 2026 with no edits	Runs with minor tweaks (env var, path)	Fails without hand-holding
Prompt design	System prompt sharp, few-shot present, constraints explicit	Adequate persona and format	Vague or missing system prompt
Robustness & fallback	Handles model errors, network errors, malformed output	Handles common errors	Crashes on the first surprise
Security discipline	No key in code, refuses out-of-scope, preserves evidence verbatim	Minor lapses	Keys committed, invents evidence

Per challenge:

    Challenge 1 — pentest-assistant: 20 points. Modelfile correctness (5), system prompt quality (5), few-shot appropriateness (5), test harness passes (5).
    Challenge 2 — ragq: 20 points. Corpus completeness (4), embedding pipeline (4), retrieval quality (4), citation of sources (4), refusal on out-of-corpus questions (4).
    Challenge 3 — recon-triage: 20 points. Nmap XML parsing (4), prompt structure (4), JSON schema validation (4), sort and rich table (4), robustness on malformed model output (4).
    Challenge 4 — finding-writer: 20 points. Claude Sonnet 5 primary path (4), Ollama fallback path (4), section validation and self-patching (4), evidence preservation (4), output file hygiene (4).
    Independent challenge (bonus): up to 20 points. Judged on scope, quality, and honest documentation of limitations.

Deductions apply for:

    Any commit that contains an API key (-20, immediate).
    Any tool that scans, probes, or attacks a target the operator has not explicitly named (-10).
    Any invented CVE number, ATT&CK ID, or evidence artifact in the finding writer output (-5 per instance).

