10. Summary and Pointer to Module 06

You just built four tools that together represent a full AI-augmented offensive-security stack:

    A specialized local model you can query offline without leaking client data.
    A retrieval layer that grounds the model in a KB you control, with citations.
    An input-parsing agent that turns unstructured scan output into ranked, structured intelligence.
    A drafting agent that turns raw findings into audit-ready client deliverables, with the cloud-model quality of Claude Sonnet 5 as the primary path and a fully local fallback for when the network or the budget disappears.

Every one of these tools shows up again in the rest of the course. Module 06 (AI-Augmented Reconnaissance) picks up right where Challenge 3 leaves off: you will chain recon-triage behind an automated Nmap pipeline, add Shodan and CT-log enrichment as new tool sources for the same model, and start driving the whole thing from pentest-assistant. Module 09 (Exploitation with AI Assist) reuses ragq as the memory layer that lets the model recall exploit primitives you have used before. Module 12 (AI-Augmented Reporting) reuses finding-writer as the core of your report-generation pipeline.

Two habits to carry forward:

    Prompts are code. Version them, test them, and treat drift in them like drift in any other library dependency. The finding-writer system prompt is the difference between a report your client will pay for and a report your engagement manager will send back.
    Local first, cloud when it helps. Client data belongs on your box. Reach for the API when the marginal quality gain justifies sending the data, and never before. Every one of the tools you just built defaults to local for a reason.

